    {
      "id": "0IECfJnwA-OVAAylWU7Cx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8844.45089602577,
      "y": -2801.2630311306093,
      "width": 1623.4786376953125,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "w2p0C7PxHocij1rwyX1el",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1135695707,
      "version": 2898,
      "versionNonce": 1230740988,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786078532696,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Absstract to agnostic frameworks systems thinking but explain from hardware 1st principles\n    * STAR\n    * end-to-end\n        - design, data, backend, model, frontend, deploy\n\nReal example:\n    Q: how would you improve performance of a pipeline\n    A: what non-functional characteristic am I improving? When running TPU computing for weeks, high availbility is imporant\n    Q: improve the latency for a data pipeline\n    A: (real responses live in the moment with limited time; joined late and hard cut off with time back)\n        - selecting too much data\n        - chunking strategies could be improved\n        - if we could use GPU, should we?\n            - CPU--i/o--GPU & clustering\n        - hardware limit to vertical computing, consider horizontal strategies\n            - consider concurrent workflows with sharding and/or parralleism for distributive compute\n        - financial constraints, sometimes you can't because of other constraints so set expectations with stakeholders\n        \nAfter some thoughts:\n    - interviewier and recruiter communicated real-examples. This interviewier and others in the past don't want hypotheticals. Signal real answers from real experience.\n    - additional approaches: memory management for less data bloat to compute faster and/or improved reads-speed\n        - improving reads by structuring data as columns instead of rows\n        - SQL Joins could be improved, Python immutability & memory/garbage management, otherwise refactor to C/C++/Rust or some other performative language\n            - database selection and other systems design considerations holistically (batch vs streaming)\n        - zero-copy (no i/o handoff between CPU/GPU and within CPU L1/L2 memory)\n        - distributive compute with clusters\n    \n    - similar to monolith vs microservices is partitioning vs sharding\n        - single: monolith, partitioning strategies (like additional subfolders in a folder)\n        - multiple: microservices, sharding",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Absstract to agnostic frameworks systems thinking but explain from hardware 1st principles\n    * STAR\n    * end-to-end\n        - design, data, backend, model, frontend, deploy\n\nReal example:\n    Q: how would you improve performance of a pipeline\n    A: what non-functional characteristic am I improving? When running TPU computing for weeks, high availbility is imporant\n    Q: improve the latency for a data pipeline\n    A: (real responses live in the moment with limited time; joined late and hard cut off with time back)\n        - selecting too much data\n        - chunking strategies could be improved\n        - if we could use GPU, should we?\n            - CPU--i/o--GPU & clustering\n        - hardware limit to vertical computing, consider horizontal strategies\n            - consider concurrent workflows with sharding and/or parralleism for distributive compute\n        - financial constraints, sometimes you can't because of other constraints so set expectations with stakeholders\n        \nAfter some thoughts:\n    - interviewier and recruiter communicated real-examples. This interviewier and others in the past don't want hypotheticals. Signal real answers from real experience.\n    - additional approaches: memory management for less data bloat to compute faster and/or improved reads-speed\n        - improving reads by structuring data as columns instead of rows\n        - SQL Joins could be improved, Python immutability & memory/garbage management, otherwise refactor to C/C++/Rust or some other performative language\n            - database selection and other systems design considerations holistically (batch vs streaming)\n        - zero-copy (no i/o handoff between CPU/GPU and within CPU L1/L2 memory)\n        - distributive compute with clusters\n    \n    - similar to monolith vs microservices is partitioning vs sharding\n        - single: monolith, partitioning strategies (like additional subfolders in a folder)\n        - multiple: microservices, sharding",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b2Q"
    },
