    {
      "id": "74s0BFzhj1ZLUJqCHI31Q",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -344.43125050873095,
      "y": 905.7652623229576,
      "width": 2305.7890625,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "yr4Hyvv2qKiW2dkxLm99V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1726318773,
      "version": 454,
      "versionNonce": 871769156,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786078473331,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Novel-problem approach (beyond Neetcode pattern-matching): restate the problem in your own words, hand-trace a small concrete example before coding, then name the technique out loud (decomposition, transformation, search/prune,\ngreedy, DP) instead of guessing at a memorized template.\n- Narrate the brute force first, before optimizing — interviewers grade the reasoning process, not just the final code. Silence reads as stuck; a wrong idea stated and self-corrected reads as senior.\n- Practice articulating complexity tradeoffs out loud, not just writing Big-O: time vs. space, average vs. worst case, amortized cost of a resize, and when a worse asymptotic complexity is the right call (small N, cache locality, simpler\ncode to maintain).\n- Advanced git — interactive rebase: squash/reorder/edit commits before opening a PR so history reads as a narrative, not a diary of fixes.\n  - `git rebase -i HEAD~n`, then pick/squash/fixup/reword each commit\n  - `git rebase --abort` to bail out safely if it goes sideways\n- Advanced git — `git bisect` to binary-search a regression: `git bisect start`, mark commits `good`/`bad`, let it walk the range — or automate the whole search with `git bisect run <test-script>`.\n- Advanced git — resolving real conflicts: read the `<<<<<<< HEAD` / `=======` / `>>>>>>>` markers as \"mine vs. theirs,\" prefer re-applying intent over blindly picking a side, and use `git rebase --onto` to salvage a branch after a\nbotched merge.\n- Advanced git — hooks (`pre-commit`, `pre-push`) for local automation: gate lint/format/tests before code ever reaches CI or review. Hooks live in `.git/hooks/`, or are shared across a team via a tool like Husky or the `pre-commit`\nframework.\n- Concurrency fundamentals: a race condition is unsynchronized access to shared mutable state; mutex (exclusive access) vs. semaphore (bounded count) vs. read-write lock; deadlock requires mutual exclusion + hold-and-wait + no\npreemption + circular wait — breaking any one prevents it.\n- Async patterns: the event loop and non-blocking I/O vs. OS threads; callback to Promise to async/await is a readability evolution, not a performance one; backpressure — a slow consumer signaling a fast producer to slow down — is\nthe concept most engineers skip until it breaks in production.\n- Distributed systems basics: CAP theorem (pick 2 of consistency/availability/partition-tolerance during a network partition); idempotency keys so retries are safe to replay; eventual consistency and read-your-own-writes; exponential\nbackoff with jitter, not fixed-interval retries.\n- Code review culture, giving feedback: comment on the diff, not the author; explicitly label blocking vs. nit vs. question; approve with comments when nothing is blocking so the change isn't stalled waiting on you.\n- Code review culture, receiving feedback: assume good intent, ask a clarifying question before pushing back, and thank the reviewer even when you disagree — the goal is a better system, not winning the thread.\n- Mentoring: ask questions that lead someone to the answer rather than handing it over; pair on the hardest 20% of a task and let them drive; assign stretch work slightly outside their comfort zone with a safety net in place.\n- Technical writing / RFCs: problem statement -> constraints -> alternatives considered (with why each was rejected) -> proposed approach -> rollout/rollback plan. Write for the reader who opens the doc six months from now with\nzero context.\n- Cross-team communication: over-communicate context before asking for help, default to async written updates over pulling people into meetings, and flag scope or timeline changes the moment you know — never let a surprise land at\nthe deadline.\n- Managing ambiguity is itself a senior skill: when scope is unclear, propose a concrete interpretation and success metric rather than waiting to be told, and timebox exploration/spikes. Senior criteria in practice = influence beyond your\nown PRs, sound judgment under uncertainty, and ownership of outcomes rather than just tasks completed.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Novel-problem approach (beyond Neetcode pattern-matching): restate the problem in your own words, hand-trace a small concrete example before coding, then name the technique out loud (decomposition, transformation, search/prune, greedy, DP) instead of guessing at a memorized template.\n- Narrate the brute force first, before optimizing — interviewers grade the reasoning process, not just the final code. Silence reads as stuck; a wrong idea stated and self-corrected reads as senior.\n- Practice articulating complexity tradeoffs out loud, not just writing Big-O: time vs. space, average vs. worst case, amortized cost of a resize, and when a worse asymptotic complexity is the right call (small N, cache locality, simpler code to maintain).\n- Advanced git — interactive rebase: squash/reorder/edit commits before opening a PR so history reads as a narrative, not a diary of fixes.\n  - `git rebase -i HEAD~n`, then pick/squash/fixup/reword each commit\n  - `git rebase --abort` to bail out safely if it goes sideways\n- Advanced git — `git bisect` to binary-search a regression: `git bisect start`, mark commits `good`/`bad`, let it walk the range — or automate the whole search with `git bisect run <test-script>`.\n- Advanced git — resolving real conflicts: read the `<<<<<<< HEAD` / `=======` / `>>>>>>>` markers as \"mine vs. theirs,\" prefer re-applying intent over blindly picking a side, and use `git rebase --onto` to salvage a branch after a botched merge.\n- Advanced git — hooks (`pre-commit`, `pre-push`) for local automation: gate lint/format/tests before code ever reaches CI or review. Hooks live in `.git/hooks/`, or are shared across a team via a tool like Husky or the `pre-commit` framework.\n- Concurrency fundamentals: a race condition is unsynchronized access to shared mutable state; mutex (exclusive access) vs. semaphore (bounded count) vs. read-write lock; deadlock requires mutual exclusion + hold-and-wait + no preemption + circular wait — breaking any one prevents it.\n- Async patterns: the event loop and non-blocking I/O vs. OS threads; callback to Promise to async/await is a readability evolution, not a performance one; backpressure — a slow consumer signaling a fast producer to slow down — is the concept most engineers skip until it breaks in production.\n- Distributed systems basics: CAP theorem (pick 2 of consistency/availability/partition-tolerance during a network partition); idempotency keys so retries are safe to replay; eventual consistency and read-your-own-writes; exponential backoff with jitter, not fixed-interval retries.\n- Code review culture, giving feedback: comment on the diff, not the author; explicitly label blocking vs. nit vs. question; approve with comments when nothing is blocking so the change isn't stalled waiting on you.\n- Code review culture, receiving feedback: assume good intent, ask a clarifying question before pushing back, and thank the reviewer even when you disagree — the goal is a better system, not winning the thread.\n- Mentoring: ask questions that lead someone to the answer rather than handing it over; pair on the hardest 20% of a task and let them drive; assign stretch work slightly outside their comfort zone with a safety net in place.\n- Technical writing / RFCs: problem statement -> constraints -> alternatives considered (with why each was rejected) -> proposed approach -> rollout/rollback plan. Write for the reader who opens the doc six months from now with zero context.\n- Cross-team communication: over-communicate context before asking for help, default to async written updates over pulling people into meetings, and flag scope or timeline changes the moment you know — never let a surprise land at the deadline.\n- Managing ambiguity is itself a senior skill: when scope is unclear, propose a concrete interpretation and success metric rather than waiting to be told, and timebox exploration/spikes. Senior criteria in practice = influence beyond your own PRs, sound judgment under uncertainty, and ownership of outcomes rather than just tasks completed.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b31"
    },
